# .backend-suite/.gitignore — scaffolded by bin/init.py, safe to commit as-is.
#
# This directory holds two very different kinds of file, and they need opposite
# treatment (see the suite's CONCEPTS.md, "Derived collaterals"):
#
#   AUTHORED — profile.json, docs.manifest.json, arch-rules.json,
#   exceptions.json, hooks.map.json, known-traps.json, component-*.json,
#   baselines/. Review these, argue about them, commit them. They are the
#   project's own declared facts and its ratchet.
#
#   REGENERABLE — everything below. Produced by running an audit, never
#   hand-written, and safe to delete at any time.
#
# Regenerable output is ignored for a reason beyond tidiness: audit findings
# quote the source they found. An audit finding's snippet is a literal line of
# this project's code, and the literals an audit greps for can be the same
# shape a hardcoded credential has. Committing findings into a client
# repository publishes code excerpts by default, which is not a decision a
# scaffolder should make silently on a team's behalf.
#
# If your project needs a finding to be durable — a baseline, an accepted
# exception — that belongs in an AUTHORED file above, deliberately, not in
# regenerable output that happens to have been committed.

findings/
evidence/
scratch/
.golden_local_ab/

# Local working state some audits write alongside their committed counterpart.
*.local.json

# Raw report /audit-layer-boundaries saves on every plain run (its --out
# default). It used to land in a top-level scripts/ directory this file could
# not reach. If an old copy is still tracked there, `git rm --cached` it.
layer-boundaries-findings.json

# Raw report engine/mockapi/audit_mock_api.py saves on every plain run (its
# --out default). It used to be <pipelines.mockApi>/findings.json at the
# project root, out of reach of this file. If an old copy is still tracked
# there, `git rm --cached` it.
mock-api-findings.json

# Deliberately NOT ignored: survey.json. It is derived and regenerable, but the
# test for ignoring something here is whether it quotes the project's source —
# survey.json records paths, stack and candidate counts, never a matched line.
# docs/CATALOG.md is the precedent: a derived file may be committed as long as
# it carries a source stamp (survey.json has `sourceStamp`) so nobody mistakes
# it for something hand-written and edits it.
